// RUF046(n, detail): findings from clauses `int-of-integer`, `int-of-rounding`. Their messages differ only in
// `detail`: each rule's head gives its clause's words. Each rule is one way a JSON `rule` can match.
// The JSON `constraints` on F, A are added to every rule.
.violation RUF046(at: node, detail: symbol) "Drop the `int(...)` {detail}."
    suppress noqa "RUF046"
RUF046(n, "wrapper and use its argument directly; the argument is already an integer") :- kind(n, "call"),
    n ~ `$F($A)`, ruf046_int_callee_family(F, "int_callee"), ruf046_arg_int(A).
RUF046(n, "wrapper; this call already returns an int. Keep it if the operand's type overrides `__round__`, `__ceil__`, `__floor__` or `__trunc__` to return a non-int") :- kind(n, "call"),
    n ~ `$F($A)`, ruf046_int_callee_family(F, "int_callee"), ruf046_arg_rounded(A).

// RUF046_int_of_integer_in_string_type(n, detail): findings from clauses `int-of-integer-in-string-type`, `int-of-rounding-in-string-type`. Their messages differ only in
// `detail`: each rule's head gives its clause's words. Each rule is one way a JSON `rule` can match.
// Columns after `at` are the metavariables the JSON's message, anchor or `each` names: one finding per distinct row.
.violation RUF046_int_of_integer_in_string_type(at: node, c: node, detail: symbol) code "RUF046" "Drop the `int(...)` {detail}."
    suppress noqa "RUF046"
RUF046_int_of_integer_in_string_type(C, n, "wrapper and use its argument directly; the argument is already an integer") :- ruf046_cast_of_int_in_type(n, C).
RUF046_int_of_integer_in_string_type(C, n, "wrapper; this call already returns an int. Keep it if the operand's type overrides `__round__`, `__ceil__`, `__floor__` or `__trunc__` to return a non-int") :- ruf046_cast_of_rounded_in_type(n, C).

